<title>NEW ZEALAND: Peters defends proposed NZ superannuation top-ups.</title>
<headline>Peters defends proposed NZ superannuation top-ups.</headline>
<dateline>AUCKLAND 1996-05-14</dateline>
<text>
<p>New Zealand Treasurer Winston Peters on Wednesday defended a proposed superannuation scheme amid questions over the affordability of proposed &quot;top ups&quot;.</p>
<p>Superannuation industry experts have said the cost of ensuring all retirees have a NZ$160,000 annuity would be around NZ$8.0 billion - $3.0 billion more than the current scheme.</p>
<p>Peters said in an interview with Radio New Zealand that the costs the critics talked of would be covered by a transitional period in which the new scheme is phased in.  </p>
<p>&quot;First of all they are addressing an issue that won't arise because we have a transition period, and that was set out clearly from the very beginning of this debate, and so their concerns are really mistaken,&quot; Peters told RNZ.</p>
<p>-- Wellington Newsroom +64-4 4734746</p>
</text>
